<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Tobacco use disorder, severe, on varenicline (F17.290)</content>. Down from twenty cigars a day to six and ready to stop. Combination nicotine replacement added to varenicline from the quit date improves the chance of success; cigars count like any other tobacco, so no cigar is safe to keep. [S]<list><item>Quit date one week from today.</item><item>From the quit date: nicotine 21 mg patch each morning and 4 mg lozenges as needed, up to 10 a day; lozenge rather than gum because gum sticks to the obturator.</item><item>Continue varenicline 1 mg twice daily.</item><item>Quitline calls weekly around the quit date; a written plan for the hours after meals and work, when he used to smoke.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Hypernasal speech and nasal leakage (R49.21)</content>. Better with the reline; the new obturator should seal fully. [S]<list><item>Speech-language therapy rebooked within two weeks.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Chronic pain at the maxillectomy site (G89.29)</content>. Pain 4 of 10 now that the flange no longer presses. [S]<list><item>Continue acetaminophen 1,000 mg three times daily and saline rinses.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Weight loss (R63.4)</content>. Weight up 0.5 kg with easier eating and supplements. [S]<list><item>Continue the soft high-protein diet and supplements.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Tobacco use (cigars a day), readiness and quit date</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>About six cigars a day; quit date in one week</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Varenicline adherence and side effects (nausea, sleep, dreams, mood)</td><td>Every visit; at least 12 weeks, extended to 24 weeks or more</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No nausea after food; milder dreams; mood steady</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Combination nicotine replacement (patch and lozenge)</td><td>From the quit date for at least 8 to 12 weeks</td><td>Ordered</td><td>21 mg patch and 4 mg lozenge from the quit date</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Behavioral support (quitline and clinic sessions)</td><td>Weekly calls around the quit date, then every two to four weeks</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Three quitline calls since enrollment; weekly calls around the quit date</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Head and neck surveillance examination (mouth, defect and neck)</td><td>Yearly by his head and neck surgeon more than five years after treatment; mouth checked at each visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Ulcer healed; no mass, induration or white or red patch; neck clear; yearly surgeon&apos;s examination booked for next month</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Head and neck imaging</td><td>Only for symptoms or signs more than five years after treatment</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>No symptoms or signs of recurrence</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Second primary cancer counseling and symptom review</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No hoarseness, trouble swallowing, cough with blood or blood in the urine; second primary risk explained again</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Obturator fit, seal and hygiene</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Relined obturator seals better; impressions for the new obturator taken last week</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Speech and swallowing (hypernasality, nasal leakage)</td><td>Every visit; speech-language therapy as needed</td><td>Deferred</td><td>Speech-language therapy has not started: the first appointment was cancelled by the clinic and not rebooked. No harm so far: speech is clearer with the reline. Corrective action: rebooked within two weeks, with a reminder call</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Dental care (high-fluoride toothpaste, cleaning every three months)</td><td>Daily toothpaste; cleaning every three months</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Using high-fluoride toothpaste nightly; the two root caries found at the consultation were filled by his dentist</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Weight and nutrition</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>58.9 kg, up 0.5 kg</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Pain score and function</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>4 of 10</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Acetaminophen daily dose (no more than 3,000 mg)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>3,000 mg a day</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Opioids</td><td>Avoid long-term opioids</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Not needed</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Stop smoking cigars completely, at a pace he chooses, using varenicline and nicotine replacement. In progress; target 2026-10-26 [S]</item><item>An obturator that seals and does not rub, so that speech is clear and liquids do not leak into his nose. In progress; target 2026-08-24 [S]</item><item>Pain at 3 of 10 or less without opioids. In progress; target 2026-10-26 [S]</item><item>Regain weight to at least 60 kg with a normal albumin. In progress; target 2026-10-26 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Initiating Pharmacologic Treatment in Tobacco-Dependent Adults. An Official American Thoracic Society Clinical Practice Guideline (2020); Tobacco Smoking Cessation in Adults, Including Pregnant Persons: Interventions (2021); NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology: Head and Neck Cancers, Version 2.2026 (follow-up and survivorship) (2026); American Cancer Society Head and Neck Cancer Survivorship Care Guideline (2016); CDC Clinical Practice Guideline for Prescribing Opioids for Pain — United States, 2022 (2022)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-